      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, I did it.' The verb for 'did' is in the masculine past tense, marked by that 'l' sound at the end.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что ты сделал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did you make?' The verb for 'did' is in the masculine past tense, marked by that 'l' sound at the end.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Я сделал ваш паштет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I made your pâté.' Max uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'pâté', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Мой паштет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My pâté?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'pâté'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Получилось один в один.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It came out exactly the same.' Notice how 'worked out' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'one'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я должен попробовать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I must taste it.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'to taste'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Даже чуть-чуть лучше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Even a little better.' Max combines 'even' with 'better' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Да ладно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No way!' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'yes' with 'all right'. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
